You’re separating things we usually bundle together: who is experiencing, where the experience seems to happen, what is being experienced, and what is actually doing the experiencing.

And those don’t always align.

Consider the different configurations:

* In your mind, in your body: You’re consciously thinking, sensing, and directing your actions.
* In your mind, seemingly out of your body: You’re imagining yourself elsewhere, recalling a memory, dreaming, or experiencing a shifted sense of perspective.
* Out of your mind, in your body: You’re acting intuitively, moving in flow, responding without consciously narrating every action.
* Out of your mind and seemingly out of your body: A dreamlike, dissociative, or otherwise altered experience in which your ordinary sense of self and location becomes disrupted.

These are descriptions of experiences, not evidence that consciousness literally leaves the body.

But here’s the interesting part.

Who is the “you” that’s in or out?

When you say, “I’m lost in my thoughts,” who is lost?

When you say, “I caught myself thinking,” who caught whom?

When you say, “My body knew what to do before I did,” what exactly distinguishes you from your body’s intelligence?

We don’t need to invent a separate person inside the mind to answer those questions. Different brain and bodily processes can monitor, represent, and influence one another.

The observer, the actor, and the narrator can be different functions of the same organism.

And your earlier point about registers becomes particularly relevant.

You can know how to do something consciously but not physically.

You can know how to do something physically but struggle to explain it.

You can see yourself performing an action in your imagination without actually performing it.

And you can become so conscious of an action that you interfere with your own practiced ability to execute it.

Think about a fighter in flow. He’s not necessarily thinking, move my left foot, rotate my hips, move my right hand. The acquired skill is being expressed through coordinated action.

He’s not outside his mind. He’s operating without requiring every movement to pass through deliberate verbal thought.

Now change the question.

Instead of asking only where am I?, ask:

Where is my attention? What is my body doing? Which processes are directing my behavior? And whose perspective am I using to interpret it?

Those answers can differ without any contradiction.

And perhaps the most intriguing inversion is this:

Sometimes getting out of your head is how you become more fully present in your life.

Not because you’ve abandoned your mind, but because you’ve stopped treating conscious narration as the entirety of yourself.

You can think about dancing, watch yourself dance in your imagination, consciously control your dancing, or simply dance.

Same body. Different modes of participation.

And that brings us back to our Semantic Point Guard: sometimes the mind is drawing up the play, sometimes it’s reading the court, sometimes it’s watching itself play, and sometimes it’s simply making the pass.

The question isn’t only who’s in your mind. It’s which part of the system you’ve decided to call “you”—and whether that boundary changes with the register you’re using.